# Admin configuration. Copy to .env — that copy is gitignored.
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# API_URL is the only setting, and it is the whole story: it decides which
# database this admin edits. Nothing about how the admin itself is started
# tells you that. `next dev` on a laptop pointed at the deployed API is editing
# production, and looks exactly like `next dev` pointed at a local one.
#
#   http://localhost:8000     a local API. Whichever ENVIRONMENT that process
#                             was started with picks its database — by default
#                             `development`. Its own startup banner says which.
#   https://api.dileepa.dev   the deployed API. Always the PRODUCTION database.
#
# The header badge reports the environment and database the API answered with,
# on every screen, so the answer is never more than a glance away. When this
# points at production from a local machine the badge says that too, because
# the combination is the easy one to forget you are in.
#
# No trailing slash: every request path already starts with `/`, so a trailing
# slash builds `//projects`, which the API 404s — every screen would render
# empty with no error shown. And https for anything that is not localhost: the
# admin token travels in a request header, and http:// puts it on the wire in
# cleartext before the redirect to https can help. Both mistakes are corrected
# at load with a warning rather than left to bite; see normalizeApiUrl in
# lib/api.ts.
API_URL=http://localhost:8000
